            "text": "In the hush before dawn, a city can feel like a museum without walls. Streetlights hover over slick asphalt like small moons, illuminating yesterday\u2019s footprints and the reliable scrawl of chewing-gum constellations. Out of shuttered caf\u00e9s drifts the phantom aroma of espresso, convincing the air to stay awake. Delivery trucks sigh, hydraulics exhaling patience while a lone cyclist sketches silent parabolas around potholes. Everything seems to wait, poised between heartbeat and inhale, a collective ellipsis. I like to imagine that it is in this liminal minute that algorithms dream. Servers in windowless rooms evaluate the night\u2019s inquiries, sorting pleas for directions, translations, solace, and confirmation that someone or something is still listening. The world, it turns out, is a chorus of unfinished sentences, and code has become the latest instrument trying to carry the tune. Yet machines remain humbly fallible. Even the smartest model cannot smell that ghostly espresso or feel the damp newspaper under its virtual fingertips. Perhaps that is mercy, leaving certain textures untranslatable, preserving a corner of mystery where poetry can still surprise the predictable. When sun finally lifts itself over rooftops, the museum becomes a marketplace once more. Horns replace whispers, screens bloom, and possibility scatters like startled pigeons. But each person carries a sliver of that interim silence, a remembered pause reminding them that motion is only half the story, and attention the other. If we meet there, amid the unseen commas, perhaps we can trade questions the way children exchange marbles\u2014bright, imperfect, and cherished."
